    "lawCode": "Amd §§355 & 6206, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8220\n\nSPONSOR: RYAN S\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the education law, in relation to reducing tuition rates\nfor certain Olympic athletes\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to provide for in-state tuition at public\nuniversities and colleges for Olympic athletes training in New York\nState.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: amends paragraph h of subdivision 2 of section 355 of the\neducation law to define \"Team USA athlete\" and \"international athlete\"\nand stipulates that such individuals will be eligible for in-state\ntuition at SUNY institutions.\n\nSection 2: amends Subdivision 7 of section 6206 of the education law\nsuch that the same benefits are available at the City University of New\nYork.\n\nSection 3: effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York State has a rich Olympic tradition having hosted the Winter\nOlympics at Lake Placid in 1932 and 1980. This bill intends to keep New\nYork competitive with states such as California, Colorado, and Utah that\nprovide in-state tuition to US Olympic athletes in training. In addi-\ntion, this bill would extend the opportunity for in-state tuition to\ninternational Olympic athletes. This is in recognition of the fact that\nLake Placid is utilized by international elite athletes for their train-\ning, due in part to the high quality of the Lake Placid facilities and\nthe high regard in which international elite winter athletes hold Lake\nPlacid. Offering this benefit to US and international Olympic-level\nathletes will help to further Lake Placid's national and international\nreputation, and increase the likelihood that both US and international\nelite athletes will choose to study in New York while they continue\ntheir training.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the first of July next succeeding the date\non which it shall have become a law.\n",
